Chitosan-assisted MOFs dispersion via covalent bonding interaction toward highly efficient removal of heavy metal ions from wastewater
a School of Materials Science & Engineering, Key Laboratory of Advanced Technologies of Materials (Ministry of Education), Southwest Jiaotong University, Chengdu 610031, China b Analytical and Testing Center, Southwest Jiaotong University, Chengdu 610031, China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
Keywords: Metal organic frameworks Chitosan Grafting Heavy metal removal Coordinatively unsaturated metal centers
## A B S T R A C T
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have been considered to be robust adsorbent for the removing heavy metal ions from wastewater due to their unique properties such as large active sites, high specific surface area and high porosity, etc., however, their practical engineering application faces the problem of serious agglomeration. In this work, a new strategy of chitosan (CS) assisting MOF dispersion was proposed to develop the new generation of MOF-based adsorbents, namely, CS grafted UiO-66-NH\(_2\) composite materials (CGUNCM). The UiO-66-NH\(_2\) was selected and it was grafted onto the main chains of CS through covalent bonding interaction with the aid of glutaraldehyde, which was totally different from the common method that grafting molecular chains on the surface of MOFs resulting in the dramatic reduction of active adsorption sites. The results show that grafting MOFs onto CS main chains not only greatly improves the dispersion of MOFs but also reserves the morphology of MOFs as much as possible. The adsorption performances toward Cu(II) and Pb(II) were intensively studied by varying adsorbate concentration, ionic strength, the contact time, adsorption temperature and pH value of solution. The results show that the composite adsorbent exhibits high adsorption efficiency and the adsorption equilibrium can be reached within \(45\,\mathrm{\min}\), and the maximum adsorption capacity toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) achieve \(364.96~\mathrm{mg/g}\) and \(556.56~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. Furthermore, the composite adsorbent shows good reusability. This work provides a new method of fabricating the MOF-based adsorbent and paves the way for the practical application of such adsorbents in wastewater treatment.
## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of industry, the demand for heavy metal minerals is also increasing. Therefore, people mine and smelt a large number of minerals to meet our needs. However, due to multiple processing and improper treatment of wastewater discharge, many heavy metals, for instance, copper, mercury, cadmium, lead, etc., enter the nature, resulting in heavy metal pollution such as water, atmosphere, and soil (Azimi et al., 2017; Efo\`me et al., 2018; Zhang et al., 2020). The pollution of heavy metal ions is seriously harmful to human health, which influences the activity of proteins and enzymes in the human body and accumulate in some organs, which causes chronic poisoning (Chen et al., 2021), that has aroused great concern in the world (Farzin et al., 2017). Among them, lead is the only trace element that the human body does not need. Excessive lead will affect children's intelligence, including speaking ability, memory and concentration, and so on (Efo\`me et al., 2018; Tokalioglu et al., 2017). Copper is an essential element for the human body, but excessive copper can cause mucosal irritation, gastric ulcer, brain damage and so on (Wen et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2018). Therefore, it is extremely necessary to eliminate Pb(II) and Cu(II) from water in terms of protection of human health.
