Numerous techniques were applied to remove heavy metals in the past decades, which includes biological methods (Putri & Syafiqa, 2019), membrane separation methods (Qiu & He, 2019; Ye et al., 2019), chemical precipitation methods (Peligno et al., 2016), electrochemical methods (Maitlo et al., 2019), ion exchange methods (Chen et al., 2019; Hosseini et al., 2020) and adsorption methods (Misra et al., 2011; Yang et al., 2019). Among these methods, the adsorption method is deemed as a promising method due to its simple operation, low operating cost, and flexibility in processing (Hu et al., 2019), and low secondary pollution. However, traditional adsorbents such as activated carbon have poor adsorption performance and poor recycling performance (Kusvuran
et al., 2012), which prompts us to develop new and efficient adsorbents.
Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) have the structural characteristics of multiple active sites, porous channels, and ultra-high specific surface area. Therefore, as an emerging material with excellent performance, MOFs are widely application prospects in environmental governance. For common medical wastewater, heavy metal ions, dye wastewater and pesticides, MOFs have excellent treatment effects compared to traditional adsorption treatment agents. Hasankola et al. (2020) reported that the zirconium-based MOF known as PCN-221 was synthesized using 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin \(\mathrm{(H_{2}TCPP)}\) as linker to remove heavy metal ions \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\), which displayed an adsorption capacity of \(233~\mathrm{mg/g}\) within \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\) of 7.1. UiO-66, as the UiO material that has attracted the most attention of researchers, has extremely excellent properties. In addition to the characteristics shared by common MOFs, UiO-66 also has strong thermochemical stability. Amino (Tang et al., 2021) \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\), nitro (Wang et al., 2020) \(\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\), hydroxyl (Sun et al., 2020) (-OH), etc. are commonly used to modify UiO-66. For example, Daliran et al. (2020) synthesized and modified UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) in three steps to synthesize pyridine triazole functionalized UiO-66, which not only had robust adsorption capacity, but also exhibited selective for \(\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{II})\). The saturated sorption capacity was \(294.1~\mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of 4.5. Xiong et al. (2020) reported a new \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\)-based adsorbent with 6-aminothiouracil to modify for lead ion removal from water, which had the advantages of high efficiency, reusability and selectivity. The saturated adsorption capacity of heavy metal ions \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) was \(386.98~\mathrm{mg/g}\) by this MOFs at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of 4.
Although the abovementioned MOFs have many advantages, they have the same disadvantages as graphene oxide (Dong et al., 2021; Majdoub et al., 2021). UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) without any carrier easily aggregates and the aggregation becomes more serious during the adsorption process due to the reduction of electrostatic repulsion (Cao et al., 2018; Lee et al., 2019), and the stacking of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) may significantly reduce its surface area and seriously affect its performance in removing pollutants (Feng et al., 2015). For all we know, it firstly reports to study the dispersion of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) for improvement of adsorption capacity of composite materials. At the same time, another shortcoming of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is inferior reusability, which makes it hardly directly used to eliminate heavy metal ions. A large number of research results show that the removal ability and reusability for removing metal ions can be improved after appropriate modification of MOFs. Saleem et al. (2016) obtained a variety of MOFs by introducing isocyanate, isothiocyanate and thiourea to functionalize UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and the efficiency of removing \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\), \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\), \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\), and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) (concentration is \(100~\mathrm{mg/L}\), respectively) was as high as \(99\%\), which greatly improved the removal ability for removing metal ions. Zhao et al. (2020) prepared the composite by in-situ growing UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) onto the nonwoven fabric, which improved the removal rate and reusability of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) in wastewater.
Chitosan (CS) is a deacetylated derivative of chitin with free \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and -OH functional groups, which have the function of adsorbing heavy metal ions. Whereas, it has fatal shortcomings such as instability in acidic medium, low mechanical strength, difficulty in separation, which restrict its utilization (Habiba et al., 2017). Therefore, CS is rarely directly used to capture metal ions, and further structure modification is needful to be aimed at changing its chemical properties and heightening its feasibility (Hosseini et al., 2021; Ifthikar et al., 2018; Sutirman et al., 2020; Vidal & Moraes, 2019) Li et al. (2017) reported that the highly cross-linked gels were obtained through chitosan and thiocarbohydrazide with formaldehyde as the cross-linking agent, which were used to remove \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\). The studies manifested that the composite materials had high adsorption abilities in a wider \(\mathrm{pH}\) range and the superior reusability for \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\).
In this work, a novel adsorbent of CS grafted UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) composite materials (CGUNCM) is proposed to highly efficiently remove heavy metal ions from wastewater. Firstly, 2-aminoterephthalic acid and \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) are used to synthesize the UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), then the cross-linking reaction
between CS and UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is induced with the aid of glutaraldehyde to prepare the composite according to the method proposed in literature (Hu et al., 2020). The presence of CS molecules is favorable for resolving the agglomeration problem of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), which augments the contact area of the CGUNCM and heavy metal ions and finally causing higher adsorption capacity. In the meantime, introducing CS can further increase the active sites, thereby further improving the adsorption capacity of the synthetic material on \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\). The results confirm that the CGUNCM is a great promise adsorbent for removal of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) from wastewater.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All raw materials can be purchased and then used directly. CS (medium viscosity, \(200{-}400~\mathrm{mPa}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}\)), 2-Aminoterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{(NH_{2}{-}B D C)}\), Zirconium chloride \(\left(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\right)\), lead nitrate, copper nitrate trihydrate were purchased in Aladdin Bio-Chem Technology Co., Ltd.(Shanghai, China). Glutaraldehyde \((50\,\mathrm{\wt\%})\), absolute ethanol, glacial acetic acid, N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), concentrated HCl were obtained from Kelong Chemical Co., Ltd. (Chengdu, China). Deionized water was provided from Hetai water system (Shanghai, China).
## 2.2. Synthesis of CGUNCM
The preparation of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) material referred to the synthesis method reported in the literature (Zhao et al., 2020). Typically, \(0.6019\,g\) \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and \(0.4521\,\mathrm{g}\,2\) -aminoterephthalic acid were dissolved in a mixture of \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) of glacial acetic acid and \(35~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF and then transferred into the \(100\;\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and crystallized at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 24 h. The reaction kettle was cooled, and the powdery product obtained was washed and then dried overnight at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under air atmosphere.
To prepare the glutaraldehyde grafted UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (UiO-66-GD) samples, \(0.4304\,\mathrm{~g~}\) UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was decentralized in \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) absolute ethanol and then \(1.2927\,\mathrm{~g~}\) glutaraldehyde \((50\,\mathrm{\wt\%})\) and 4 drops of glacial acetic acid were added to the dispersion (Hu et al., 2020). Then the system was refluxed at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{10\mathrm{~h~}}\). The reaction system was cooled, and the powdery product obtained was washed and then dried overnight at \(70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under air atmosphere.
To prepare the CGUNCM samples, \(0.1024\,\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{CS}\) was dissolved in \(5\,\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water. \(0.2024\,\mathrm{g}\) UiO-66-GD was decentralized in the dispersion and then 4 drops of glacial acetic acid were added to mixture. Then the system was maintained at \(70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{15\mathrm{~h~}}\) and then cooled to room temperature. The product obtained was washed and then dried overnight at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under air atmosphere to obtain the CGUNCM samples. The fabrication procedures of the CGUNCM are schematically represented in Fig. 1.
