## 2.4. Adsorption measurements
To research the influence of CGUNCM on the adsorption property under different conditions, a series of adsorption experiments were performed. The effects of the initial pH within the scope of 2.0–6.0 and the \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\)ionic strength in the range of \(0{-}0.3\;\mathrm{mol/L}\) for removing heavy metal ions were respectively explored. To study the impact of temperature on the adsorption property, the adsorption system was adjusted within the scope of \(10{-}40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Adsorption thermodynamics studies were performed by altering concentrations of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\). Adsorption kinetic experiments were carried out by different time intervals. In order to quest the recycle of CGUNCM, the composite that has adsorbed \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) (or \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\)) was desorbed with \(0.1\ \mathrm{mol/L}\) HCl, then it was performed in the another time of adsorption experiment. The details of adsorption capacity \((q)\) is given in SI and thermodynamic models, kinetic models and thermodynamic parameters are given in SII.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and structural characterization
The XRD patterns of MOFs are provided in Fig. 2a. The XRD pattern of the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) appears new strong diffraction peaks at 2θ of \(7.4^{\circ}\), \(8.6^{\circ}\), \(12.1^{\circ}\) and \(25.7^{\circ}\), which can be attributed to the [111], [002], [022] and [224] crystal planes of the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\), respectively (Du et al., 2019). The presence of such sharp diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) indicates that the crystal structure of the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) formed intact without major defects. The crystal structure of as-synthesized \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) has been confirmed in accordance with previous literature reports (Chavan et al., 2014). The sharper the peak shape indicates the higher the crystallinity of the composite. The XRD patterns of glutaraldehyde-modified MOFs (UiO-66-GD) and chitosan-grafted MOFs (CGUNCM) are basically the same as those of the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\), which proves that introducing functional groups does not destroy the crystal structure of
UiO-66-NH\(_2\). After being cross-linked with glutaraldehyde, the relative content of MOFs in the sample decreases, leading to the reduction of the peak intensity of UiO-66-GD. With the addition of chitosan, the peak strength of MOFs decreases due to the further decrease of MOFs content (Xian et al., 2015). This clearly indicates that UiO-66-NH\(_2\) has been grafted onto chitosan molecules.
The synthesis processes of the CGUNCM are further comprehended by FTIR spectra. All the relevant data are consistent with the literature reports (Yuan et al., 2017), as shown in Fig. 2b. The \(3452\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) signal is classified into the nonsymmetric stretching vibration of the -NH\(_2\) group. A relatively strong peak appears at \(1569\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which can be attributed to the bending vibration of the -NH\(_2\) group. The characteristic stretching vibration peaks of -NH\(_2\) on the MOFs emerges at \(1427\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1276\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Tang et al., 2021), and the absorption bands at \(766\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(671\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) classify into the C—N and Zr—O bond, respectively. The 1658 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) signal can be ascribed to C=O stretching vibration (Chavan et al., 2014). These evidences suggest that UiO-66-NH\(_2\) crystals are successfully prepared. Meanwhile, the \(1621\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be attributed to stretching vibration peaks of C=N bond, which not only proves the formation of Schiff base but also confirms the successful introduction of glutaraldehyde (UiO-66-GD). The absorption band at \(1715\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to the C—O stretching vibration of the saccharide structure (Kardanpour et al., 2015; Yuan et al., 2017). Besides that, the strong characteristic peaks of CS at the range of \(750{-}1250\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were found in the as-prepared CGUNCM, demonstrating that chitosan is successfully grafted by the UiO-66-GD. The characteristic absorption peak of C—H on the aldehyde group is assigned at \(2844\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), indicating that there are excessive aldehyde groups after the reaction of UiO-66-GD with chitosan. These above consequences prove that UiO-66-NH\(_2\) has been grafted onto chitosan molecules with the aid of glutaraldehyde.
Fig. 2c shows the thermal weight loss analysis of all samples. It can be known from the graph that the TGA curve of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) shows a two-step weight loss (Daliran et al., 2020). The first stage of weight loss at \(30{-}120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is caused by the adsorbed water vapor in the pores and the volatilization of untreated solvent molecules. The second stage of weight loss (after \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) is attributed to the collapse of skeleton for the material, but the collapse of skeleton for the UiO-66-GD just occurs at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which indicates that the introduction of glutaraldehyde contributes to the improvement of the thermal stability of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\). For the CGUNCM, the rapid weight loss at \(150{-}300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is mainly caused by the decomposition of chitosan and the weight loss at temperature higher than \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) indicates the occurrence of the skeleton collapse of the material. The total weight loss of the CGUNCM, which is \(85.57\%\), is higher than the samples before grafting, indicating that MOFs are successfully grafted onto chitosan (Habiba et al., 2017). The above results further confirm that the CGUNCM is successfully obtained and the prepared CGUNCM exhibits high thermal stability.
The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) and CGUNCM at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) were measured to further find out the porosity of the material. Adsorption isotherms can be generally classified into six types (Bin Ismail et al., 2016), and different types of adsorption isotherms correspond to materials with different structures. As shown in Fig. 2d, the isotherms of two samples fit to type I isotherms. In general, the isotherms of microporous materials such as molecular sieve, microporous silica gel and zeolite present this type, which belongs to a single-layer reversible adsorption, and the adsorption capacity is controlled by the pore volume. At a higher relative pressure \((\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}>0.95)\), the rapid increase of isotherms of the samples can reflect the presence of large pores or particle accumulation holes (Daliran et al., 2020; Zhao et al., 2020). The total pore volume and BET surface area of the CGUNCM are \(0.1675\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) and \(363.938\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively, which are lower than the total pore volume \((0.3429\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) and BET surface area \((781.516\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Table S1). This may be related to the non-porous nature of chitosan and the larger mass fraction in the CGUNCM, which conclusively leads to a relatively small surface area per unit mass. The above results all indicate that chitosan has been triumphantly grafted UiO-66-NH\(_2\) to form the composite CGUNCM. At the same time, the advantages of the larger specific surface area of MOFs are retained as much as possible.
