The surface morphologies of MOFs before and after being crosslinked with CS were characterized by SEM (Fig. 3). From the SEM images (Fig. 3a and b) one can see that the prepared UiO-66-NH\(_2\) has regular octahedral structure (Daliran et al., 2020; Tang et al., 2021), while the size is relatively uniform and the particle size is among \(100{-}200\,\mathrm{nm}\). In Fig. 3c and d, the edges and corners of the octahedron become rough, which proves the successful introduction of glutaraldehyde. As shown in Fig. 3e and f, the MOFs in the CGUNCM exhibit the similar morphology to that of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\), revealing that the introduction of chitosan does not change the crystal structure of MOFs. Furthermore, it can be also seen that MOFs exhibit relatively homogeneous dispersion in the composite. The EDS results of the composite are shown in Fig. S1. It suggests that the Zr element is uniformly distributed in the composite, further indicating that the MOF particles are uniformly present in the composite sample.
## 3.2. Batch adsorption experiments
The adsorption capacities toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) by the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) powder, CS and the CGUNCM were comparatively investigated in the same conditions to explore the adsorption performance of the CGUNCM. It can be seen from Fig. 4 that the CGUNCM has better removal ability for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) than the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) and CS, and the adsorption capacities are even much higher than the sum of the adsorption capacities of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) and CS. There may be two reasons: (1) due to the introduction of CS, the composite has more active sites; (2) MOFs in the CGUNCM are more evenly dispersed, so that the composite has a larger contact area with heavy metal ions, and finally causes that the adsorption capacity of the composite for heavy metal ions is greatly increased. Therefore, it is deduced that the dispersion of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) has a greater impact on the adsorption capacity of the composite for heavy metal ions.
\(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) by the CGUNCM is basically unchanged.
The temperature of the adsorption system also has a significant impact on the adsorption capacity of the adsorbent for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\), because temperature affects the diffusion rate of ions, the probability of effective collision, and the adsorption equilibrium constant. Therefore, the adsorption experiments of CGUNCM in different temperatures ranging from \(283.15\,\mathrm{~K~}\) to \(313.15\,\mathrm{~K~}\) were studied. The adsorption capacity of the CGUNCM under different temperatures, Van't Hoff plots and calculated values of the thermodynamic parameters are shown in Figs. 5c, S2 and Table S2, respectively. During the adsorption process of composite for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\), \(\Delta G<0\), proving that the reaction can be spontaneous, and the higher the temperature, the greater the absolute value of \(\Delta G\), the greater the driving force, so the higher temperature is conducive to the adsorption reaction. The adsorption enthalpy \(\Delta H>0\) indicates that the adsorption reaction is endothermic process. \(\Delta S>0\), indicating that the adsorption of the CGUNCM for heavy metal ions leads to an augment in the degree of disorder. It agrees with the experimental results, which the adsorption amount increases slightly as temperature increases. It may be the increase in the activity of the surface functional groups due to the increase in temperature and the Brownian motion is aggravated by increasing the temperature (Zhao et al., 2020), which is benefit to the diffusion of heavy metal ions to the surface of the adsorbent.
The impact of salt effect during the actual adsorption course is one of the factors that must be considered. Therefore, it is necessary to research
the influence of salt effect on the adsorption capacity of the CGUNCM. The adsorption experiments on the CGUNCM in different \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) concentrations \((0{-}0.3\,\mathrm{\mol/L})\) of the initial solution had been carried out (Fig. 5d). With the increase of \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) concentration, the adsorption capacity of the CGUNCM for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) decreased significantly (Wan et al., 2010). When \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) concentration increases, the competitive adsorption of heavy metal ions and \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) in the adsorption process is enhanced so that the active sites of the composites, which can complex with heavy metal ions, are reduced. In addition, the increased \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) concentration can be beneficial to agglomeration of the CGUNCM on account of compacting the bioelectronic layer and/or electrostatic interactions and decreasing the electrostatic repulsion of electronegative samples (Musso et al., 2019). In consequence, the amount of effective active sites are reduced and the removal capacity of \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) decrease accordingly.
Study on adsorption thermodynamics, mainly including Langmuir model and Freundlich (SII), is favorable for understanding the interaction between adsorbent and adsorbate in the adsorption process. In this work, adsorption experiments on the CGUNCM for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) in the different initial solution concentrations \((50{-}1000~\mathrm{mg/L})\) had been carried out, and the variation of adsorption capacity with increasing concentrations are illustrated in Fig. 6a. When the initial concentration of heavy metal ions are relatively lower, there is not enough driving force, so that heavy metal ions in the solution can only be complexed with the functional groups on the surface of the CGUNCM, resulting in a low adsorption capacity. As the initial concentration of heavy metal ions continues to increase, the concentration differences between solution containing heavy metal ions and the interior of the CGUNCM also increase, so the provided driving force, which the heavy metal ions move from the liquid phase to the solid phase, is also increasing. The larger the concentration gradient is, the stronger the driving force is, which is more beneficial to the diffusion into the internal of the CGUNCM for heavy metal ions, leading to increase of the adsorption capacity. When the concentration of \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) increases to a certain level, most of the active centers of the CGUNCM are occupied, making the increase of the adsorption capacity begin to slow (Wan et al., 2010). To further study the adsorption performance, Langmuir model and Freundlich model (SII) are used to suit the adsorption behavior of the CGUNCM under different \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) concentrations. The resultant thermodynamic curves and parameters are illustrated in Fig. S3 and Table S3, respectively. After calculation, all the correlation coefficient of the Langmuir model are greater than 0.99 and their theoretical saturated adsorption capacity are close to the experimental data, and the sum of the square of the error (SSE), sum of absolute error (SAE) and chi square \((\chi^{2})\) of the Langmuir model, between the experimental data and calculated values, are smaller than the Freundlich model, which shows that the Langmuir model is more suitable for the adsorption behavior of the \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}\)
(II) by the CGUNCM. The theoretical saturated adsorption capacity are \(364.96\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(555.56\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) for \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\), respectively, which are superior to other adsorbents previously reported (Fig. 6c and d) (Wan et al., 2010; Hao et al., 2012; Wang et al., 2016; Tokalioglu et al., 2017; Efome et al., 2018; Mehdinia et al., 2018; Shariful et al., 2018; Kumar et al., 2019; Pavithra et al., 2021), indicating that the CGUNCM exhibits excellent adsorption performance toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\). These consequences prove that it has homogeneous adsorption sites on the surface of CGUNCM and heavy metal ions are adsorbed with monolayer adsorption on the CGUNCM (El-Khaiary et al., 2010; Song et al., 2019).
Adsorption kinetics mainly studies the correlation between adsorption time and adsorbent. The results are illustrated in Fig. 6b. With the extension of the adsorption time, the adsorption capacity of the CGUNCM toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) increases (Song et al., 2019). The adsorption rates of \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) are extremely fast and the adsorption capacity is close to the equilibrium adsorption capacity in only 5 min. After \(5\;\mathrm{min}\), as the adsorption time increases, the increase in the adsorption capacity is significantly reduced, and the adsorption equilibrium is basically reached at \(80\;\mathrm{min}\). In order to further study the adsorption performance, the adsorption process was fitted according to the kinetic model (SII). The higher the correlation between the experimental data and the predicted value of the kinetic model are, the better the model could fit the adsorption of heavy metal ions by materials. The resultant kinetics curves and parameters are respectively illustrated in Fig. S4 and Table S4. The pseudo second-order model better fits the process of adsorbing \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) by the CGUNCM, because the correlation coefficients of the pseudo second-order model \(\left(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2}=0.9992\right.\), \(\left.\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{Pb}}^{2}=0.9999\right)\) are much higher than the correlation coefficients of the pseudo first-order model and the intraparticle diffusion model, and the theoretical maximum adsorption capacity are more close to the experimental data. The sum of the square of the error (SSE), sum of absolute error (SAE) and chi square \(\left(\chi^{2}\right)\) of the pseudo second-order model, between the experimental data and calculated values, are smaller than those obtained through the pseudo first-order model, indicating that chemical adsorption dominates the adsorption behavior of the CGUNCM for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\). In addition, the curve fitted by the intra-particle diffusion (Fig. S3c) model does not pass through the origin, indicating that it's not the dominating step. Furthermore, the low \(k_{id}\) value means that the surface adsorption as a participator limits the adsorption rate of the CGUNCM on heavy metal ions. Therefore, it may come to the conclusion that the adsorption can be divided into two stages: the first stage belongs to the rapid adsorption stage, in which the initial concentration of the solution is higher and the number of active sites on the surface of the adsorbent is large, so the adsorption speed is fast; the second stage reaches the adsorption equilibrium, and the adsorbate binds to the active site to the maximum extent.
