Furthermore, the adsorption selectivity of the CGUNCM toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) from the simulated wastewater containing other heavy metal ions, such as \(\mathrm{Cr(III)}\), \(\mathrm{Hg(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn(II)}\), was also measured. During the adsorption measurement, the above five heavy metal ions were simultaneously present in the simulated wastewater and the concentration for each heavy metal ion was maintained at \(300\;\mathrm{mg/L}\). The comparison of the adsorption capacities toward these heavy metal ions is shown in Fig. S5. It is clearly seen that the CGUNCM exhibits a certain adsorption selectivity toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) among these heavy metal ions. The most interesting phenomenon is that the CGUNCM can simultaneously remove various heavy metal ions and the total adsorption capacity achieves \(619.34\;\mathrm{mg/g}\), which is much higher than the theoretical maximum adsorption capacities toward \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) \((364.96\;\mathrm{mg/g})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) \((555.56\;\mathrm{mg/g})\) obtained in this work. This indirectly confirms the great potential application of the CGUNCM in wastewater treatment.
In order to explore the ability of the CGUNCM to remove heavy metal ions \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) in practical applications. The removal experiment was performed when the \(\mathrm{pH}\) was 5.5, the temperature was \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\)concentration was \(0.1\;\mathrm{mol/L}\), and the \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb(II)}\) concentrations were \(30\;\mathrm{mg/L}\) and \(50\;\mathrm{mg/L}\), respectively. The designed experimental device is shown in Fig. 7a. The CGUNCM was placed between two absorbent cottons and the solution containing heavy metal ions
flows out from top to bottom. The flow rate was controlled by the knob of the filter column and the flow rate was set at \(2~\mathrm{mL/min}\) . The concentration of effluent was collected and tested. The results are shown in the Fig. 7b. For \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) , the CGUNCM shows excellent adsorption efficiency. After filtration, the concentration of \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) is reduced from 29.84 to \(0.59\,\mathrm{mg/L}\) , with an efficiency of \(98.80\%\) . For \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) , the concentration is reduced from 51.22 to \(0.74\:\mathrm{mg/L}\) after filtration, corresponding to an adsorption efficiency of \(98.51\%\) , which is lower than the permitted concentration \((1.0~\mathrm{mg/L})\) of the national discharge standard of China (GB 20426-2006). The results confirm that the CGUNCM is a great promise adsorbent for removal of \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) from wastewater.
## 3.3. Adsorption mechanisms
The adsorption mechanisms of the CGUNCM toward \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{b}\) (II) includes physical and chemical adsorption. Nonetheless, chemical adsorption plays a major role in removing \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) , while physical adsorption is an auxiliary role. The characterization of the CGUNCM before and after adsorbing \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) were further analyzed by FTIR and XPS. As shown in Fig. 8a, stretching vibrations of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) and \(\mathrm{N-H}\) appear at \(3392~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and stretching vibration of \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}\) appears at \(1723~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , proving that aldehyde and amino groups exist in the CGUNCM. After adsorbing \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) , all the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{N-H}\) , O H, \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{O}\) and \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) change to a certain degree. The characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) and \(\mathrm{N-H}\) of \(\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C G U N C M}\) turn to 3379
\(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and the characteristic peaks of \(\scriptstyle{C=\mathrm{O}}\) turns to \(1709\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , and the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) and \(\mathrm{N-H}\) of \(\mathrm{Pb/CGUNCM}\) turn to 3379 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and the characteristic peaks of \(\scriptstyle{C=\mathrm{O}}\) turns to \(1714~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , presenting that these functional groups have strong binding force with Cu (II) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) , which may be result of the electrostatic surface complexation (Ifthikar et al., 2017). Through the Zeta potential measured at different pH values as shown in Fig. 5b, it can be found that the isoelectric point of the CGUNCM is 2.35, which is the result of the protonation of the amino group and the hydroxyl group of the composite. When the pH value is greater than 2.35, the surface of CGUNCM is negatively charged (Efome et al., 2018), which is beneficial to adsorb \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) (Zhang et al., 2019) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) . It is consistent with the experimental results.
XPS analysis of the CGUNCM before and after adsorbing \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) were performed to explore the chemical changes in the surrounding environment of the element for the sake of better revealing the adsorption mechanism of the CGUNCM for \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) . The XPS spectra of the CGUNCM, Cu/CGUNCM and Pb/CGUNCM are compared, the new peaks of \(\mathrm{~Cu~}\,2\mathrm{p}\) in the \(\mathrm{{Cu/CGUNCM}}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb~4f}\) in the \(\mathrm{Pb}/\) CGUNCM (Fig. 8b) are clearly presented. The binding energies of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) \(2{\tt p}3/2\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}\,2{\tt p}1/2\) are assigned to \(933.52\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(953.75\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. S6) and binding energies of \(\mathrm{Pb}\,4\mathrm{f}7/2\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}\,4\mathrm{f}5/2\) are assigned to \(138.31\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(143.17~\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. S7), proving that \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) are successfully adsorbed by the CGUNCM. The XPS spectra of N 1 s and O 1 s are also carried out so that can well uncover the adsorption mechanism of CGUNCM for heavy metal ions. As illustrated in Fig. 8c, N 1 s spectrum
can be split into two peaks -NH and \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) (Wang et al., 2015). Comparing the spectra of the CGUNCM, \(\mathrm{Cu/CGUNCM}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb/}\) CGUNCMS, the binding energy of N 1s changes from \(399.04~\mathrm{eV}\) to \(398.94~\mathrm{eV}\) (Cu/CGUNCM) or \(398.84~\mathrm{eV}\) (Pb/CGUNCM), the content of -NH augments from \(15.20\%\) to \(26.43\%\) (Cu/CGUNCM) or \(22.78\%\) (Pb/ CGUNCM), and the content of \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) decreases from \(84.80\%\) to \(73.57\%\) (Cu/CGUNCM) or \(77.22\%\) (Pb/CGUNCM), indicating the -NH and \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) have complexing action with heavy metal ions, and the results are consistent with those of FT-IR analysis. Besides, Fig. 8d presents that O 1 s spectrum can be split into three peaks \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\) , \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{-}}\mathsf{O}\) and O-Metal (Luo et al., 2018). After adsorption of \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) , the binding energy of O 1 s changes from \(532.44\;\mathrm{eV}\) to \(531.96\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Cu/CGUNCM) and 532.04 \(\mathrm{eV}\) (Pb/CGUNCM), respectively, which it reveals that \(\mathrm{O}^{2-}\) is involved in the entire adsorption process. At the same time, the content of O-Metal augments from \(20.78\%\) to \(48.43\%\) (Cu/CGUNCM) and \(29.96\%\) (Pb/ CGUNCM), respectively. On the contrary, the relative content of \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{-}}\mathsf{O}\) decreases from \(47.11\%\) to \(34.47\%\) (Cu/CGUNCM) and \(42.14\%\) (Pb/ CGUNCM) and \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\) drops from \(32.11\%\) to \(17.10\%\) (Cu/CGUNCM) or \(29.96\%\) (Pb/CGUNCM), and evidencing that there are chemical bonds between heavy metal ions and \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{-}}\mathsf{O}\) , \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{-}}\mathsf{O}\) in CGUNCM. In fact, when \(\mathrm{O}^{2-}\) is complexed with \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) , the O atom provides electrons to heavy metal ions, resulting in decrease of its electron cloud density, thereby causing the binding energy to move forward. But at the same time, the binding energy of O moves to a lower energy value, indicating that the coordination of heavy metal ions with oxygen-containing functional groups on the surface of the CGUNCM is not the only adsorption mechanism. It is worth noting that the bridging O atoms in the Zr-O-Zr structure are likely to be covalently bonded to heavy metal ions, leading to a decrease in the binding energy of O atoms. A similar phenomenon exists in the adsorption of \(\mathrm{{Cr}(I I I)}\) on zeolite (Black et al., 2004). Therefore, the adsorption mechanisms of the CGUNCM for \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) are presented in the Fig. 9.
## 3.4. Reusability
In order to meet the requirements of practical application, besides good adsorption performance, the regeneration performance of adsorbent is very important. Here, adsorption-desorption experiments on the CGUNCM were performed. The CGUNCM after adsorbing heavy metal ions was placed in a \(0.1\;\mathrm{mol/L}\) disodium edetate solution and desorbed by shaking for \(4\;\mathrm{h}\), and the relationship between desorption rate and time after adsorption of heavy metals by the CGUNCM is shown in Fig. S8. After centrifugal separation, washing and drying, the regenerated CGUNCM was obtained for the next adsorption. As illustrated in Fig. 10, the adsorption capacity of the CGUNCM for \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) gradually reduces with the increase of the number of cycles. It may be
due to the destruction of a small part of the framework structure after desorption or part of the active sites occupied by heavy metal ions, resulting in the reduced binding sites. However, it is worth noting that even after 5 cycles, the adsorption capacities still reach \(74.6\%\) and \(74.8\%\) of the first adsorption capacity, respectively, indicating that the CGUNCM has good reusability.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the CGUNCM has been successfully prepared by grafting \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) onto chitosan. The morphological characterizations show that \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is more uniformly dispersed on chitosan and the prepared CGUNCM has good thermal stability. Through the study of its adsorption performance, it is found that the adsorption of heavy metal ions \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) by the CGUNCM conforms to the Langmuir model and the quasi-secondary adsorption model, and the adsorption is a spontaneous process, and the theoretical maximum adsorption capacities toward \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) by the CGUNCM reach \(364.96~\mathrm{mg/g}\) and \(555.56~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. After recycling experiments, it is found that CGUNCM has good reusability. Therefore, The CGUNCM has the advantages of convenient synthesis, strong adsorption capacity for heavy metal ions, and good reusability, and it has a wide range of applications in wastewater treatment.
